yright-law-dc80620c1c226a816048b87fb30309c4?utm_campaign=mb&amp;utm_medium=newsletter&amp;utm_source=morning_brew</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://apnews.com/article/uk-musicians-protest-ai-copyright-law-dc80620c1c226a816048b87fb30309c4?utm_campaign=mb&amp;utm_medium=newsletter&amp;utm_source=morning_brew</guid><pubDate>Mon, 03 Mar 2025 16:56:35 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[I never knew the median voter theorem was]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/GlEtYuRawAI7ktT.jpg:large"></p><p>Richard Hanania (@RichardHanania) on X</p><p>Well that’s encouraging.</p><p>URL: <a href="https://x.com/RichardHanania/status/1896346824639971744">https://x.com/RichardHanania/status/1896346824639971744</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/03/monday-assorted-links-498.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=monday-assorted-links-498">Monday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://x.com/RichardHanania/status/1896346824639971744</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://x.com/RichardHanania/status/1896346824639971744</guid><pubDate>Mon, 03 Mar 2025 16:56:35 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[How is Australian state capacity doing?]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/GlGJ2rZbMAABKrj.png:large"></p><p>Joseph Noel Walker (@JosephNWalker) on X</p><p>Australia has relatively high state capacity. That is, our administrative state is unusually effective. This deserves study, but I was surprised to find that very little had been directly written on the topic. So the economist Peter Bowers and I produced a literature review.🧵</p><p>URL: <a href="https://x.com/JosephNWalker/status/1896460898828783866">https://x.com/JosephNWalker/status/1896460898828783866</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/03/monday-assorted-links-498.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=monday-assorted-links-498">Monday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://x.com/JosephNWalker/status/1896460898828783866</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://x.com/JosephNWalker/status/1896460898828783866</guid><pubDate>Mon, 03 Mar 2025 16:56:35 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[Why do attractive female servers earn more in tips?]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/GlEccSDboAEwHUW.jpg:large"></p><p>Macken (@MackenMurphy) on X</p><p>“Attractive servers earn approximately $1261 more per year in tips... the primary driver of which is female customers tipping attractive females more than unattractive females.” Counter-intuitive both from a female-female competition perspective and a mating market perspective.</p><p>URL: <a href="https://x.com/MackenMurphy/status/1896328198398652825">https://x.com/MackenMurphy/status/1896328198398652825</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/03/monday-assorted-links-498.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=monday-assorted-links-498">Monday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://x.com/MackenMurphy/status/1896328198398652825</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://x.com/MackenMurphy/status/1896328198398652825</guid><pubDate>Mon, 03 Mar 2025 16:56:35 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[Planned architecture for Tirana]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.dezeen.com/2025/02/13/tirana-albania-unusual-architecture/">https://www.dezeen.com/2025/02/13/tirana-albania-unusual-architecture/</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/03/monday-assorted-links-498.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=monday-assorted-links-498">Monday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://www.dezeen.com/2025/02/13/tirana-albania-unusual-architecture/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.dezeen.com/2025/02/13/tirana-albania-unusual-architecture/</guid><pubDate>Mon, 03 Mar 2025 16:56:35 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[A guy who doesn’t understand Paul Verhoeven]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/Gk-QkTHXAAAOFxS.jpg:large"></p><p>Schwalbe⚡️ (@realhumanschwab) on X</p><p>Verhoeven did *intend* to make a movie that said “war makes fascists of us all” but he failed on every level because he doesn’t understand fascism, war or how propaganda works. 1/</p><p>URL: <a href="https://x.com/realhumanschwab/status/1895914253506527284">https://x.com/realhumanschwab/status/1895914253506527284</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/03/sunday-assorted-links-506.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=sunday-assorted-links-506">Sunday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://x.com/realhumanschwab/status/1895914253506527284</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://x.com/realhumanschwab/status/1895914253506527284</guid><pubDate>Sun, 02 Mar 2025 16:55:32 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[Context is that which is scarce, AI edition]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1528128033038602240/cMlQKeTG_200x200.jpg"></p><p>Linus (@thesephist) on X</p><p>Hypothesis: information work is overwhelmingly bottlenecked on availability of high-signal context more than by correct inference over the context. If right, implies higher ROI-per-flop of context building over pure logical inference. h/t @anandnk24</p><p>URL: <a href="https://x.com/thesephist/status/1895887696268288119?s=46">https://x.com/thesephist/status/1895887696268288119?s=46</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/03/sunday-assorted-links-506.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=sunday-assorted-links-506">Sunday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://x.com/thesephist/status/1895887696268288119?s=46</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://x.com/thesephist/status/1895887696268288119?s=46</guid><pubDate>Sun, 02 Mar 2025 16:55:32 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[the most rapid change in human communication ever]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/Gk90PjWW4AA-Sam.jpg:large"></p><p>Ethan Mollick (@emollick) on X</p><p>The past 18 months have seen the most rapid change in human written communication ever By. September 2024, 18% of financial consumer complaints, 24% of press releases, 15% of job postings &amp; 14% of UN press releases showed signs of LLM writing. And the method undercounts true use</p><p>URL: <a href="https://x.com/emollick/status/1895861786743881867?s=46&t=fSypFxt84bBHId-do9r5Ag">https://x.com/emollick/status/1895861786743881867?s=46&t=fSypFxt84bBHId-do9r5Ag</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/03/sunday-assorted-links-506.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=sunday-assorted-links-506">Sunday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://x.com/emollick/status/1895861786743881867?s=46&amp;t=fSypFxt84bBHId-do9r5Ag</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://x.com/emollick/status/1895861786743881867?s=46&amp;t=fSypFxt84bBHId-do9r5Ag</guid><pubDate>Sun, 02 Mar 2025 16:55:32 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[the power of DeepSeek?]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1225476100547063809/53jSWs7z_200x200.jpg"></p><p>Jeffrey Emanuel (@doodlestein) on X</p><p>OK, so now we know just how much more efficient DeepSeek is for inference in terms of total tokens per second processed. They are doing roughly 7-8x more tokens per second on an H-800 (a crippled, export-control version on the H-100) than the open-source state of the art on H-100</p><p>URL: <a href="https://x.com/doodlestein/status/1895825001687011580?s=46">https://x.com/doodlestein/status/1895825001687011580?s=46</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/03/sunday-assorted-links-506.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=sunday-assorted-links-506">Sunday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://x.com/doodlestein/status/1895825001687011580?s=46</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://x.com/doodlestein/status/1895825001687011580?s=46</guid><pubDate>Sun, 02 Mar 2025 16:55:32 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[“…]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0304407620302773">https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0304407620302773</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2025/03/sunday-assorted-links-506.html?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=sunday-assorted-links-506">Sunday assorted links</a></p>]]></description><link>https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0304407620302773</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0304407620302773</guid><pubDate>Sun, 02 Mar 2025 16:55:32 GMT</pubDate></item><item><title><![CDATA[Partial liberation from maternal human capital is a significant reason why American became such a land of opportunity]]></title><description><![CDATA[<p><img src="https://pbs.twimg.com/media/Gk-zsAXXwAAm445.jpg:large"></p><p>Lukas Althoff (@AlthoffLukas) on X</p><p>How did the US become a land of opportunity? In a new paper, we show that the country’s pioneering role in mass education was key to its rise in intergenerational mobility from 1850 to 1950. “America’s Rise in Human Capital Mobility”
